            ]
          },
          "worked": "$$(a+b\\sqrt2)^2=a^2+2b^2+2ab\\sqrt2$$\nSince $a,b$ are rational and $\\sqrt2$ is irrational, compare coefficients:\n$$\\begin{align*}a^2+2b^2&=22\\\\2ab&=-12\\\\ab&=-6\\\\b&=-\\frac6a\\end{align*}$$\nHere $a\\ne0$. Substituting for $b$ gives\n$$\\begin{align*}a^2+\\frac{72}{a^2}&=22\\\\a^4-22a^2+72&=0\\\\(a^2-4)(a^2-18)&=0\\end{align*}$$\nThus $a^2=4$ or $a^2=18$. The latter gives irrational $a=\\pm3\\sqrt2$, so it is excluded. With $b=-\\frac{6}{a}$, the solutions are $(a,b)=(2,-3)$ or $(-2,3)$.\n$$\\begin{align*}(2-3\\sqrt2)^2&=4+18-12\\sqrt2\\\\(2-3\\sqrt2)^2&=22-12\\sqrt2\\end{align*}$$\nNegating the whole bracket gives the same square, verifying the second pair.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 154
