          },
          "worked": "$$(a+b\\sqrt2)^2=a^2+2b^2+2ab\\sqrt2$$\nComparing rational and surd coefficients gives\n$$\\begin{align*}a^2+2b^2&=41\\\\2ab&=-24\\\\ab&=-12\\\\b&=-\\frac{12}{a}\\end{align*}$$\nHere $a\\ne0$. Substituting for $b$ gives\n$$\\begin{align*}a^2+\\frac{288}{a^2}&=41\\\\a^4-41a^2+288&=0\\\\(a^2-9)(a^2-32)&=0\\end{align*}$$\nThus $a^2=9$ or $a^2=32$. The latter gives irrational $a=\\pm4\\sqrt2$, so it is excluded. With $b=-\\frac{12}{a}$, the solutions are $(a,b)=(3,-4)$ or $(-3,4)$.\n$$\\begin{align*}(3-4\\sqrt2)^2&=9+32-24\\sqrt2\\\\(3-4\\sqrt2)^2&=41-24\\sqrt2\\end{align*}$$\nNegating the whole bracket gives the same square, verifying the second pair.",
