          "worked": "$$(1+a\\sqrt2+b\\sqrt3)^2=1+2a^2+3b^2+2a\\sqrt2+2b\\sqrt3+2ab\\sqrt6$$\nThe numbers $1,\\sqrt2,\\sqrt3,\\sqrt6$ are linearly independent over the rationals. Comparing coefficients of $\\sqrt2$ and $\\sqrt3$ gives\n$$\\begin{align*}2a&=2\\\\a&=1\\\\2b&=-4\\\\b&=-2\\end{align*}$$\nCheck the other two coefficients:\n$$\\begin{align*}1+2a^2+3b^2&=1+2(1)^2+3(-2)^2\\\\1+2a^2+3b^2&=15\\\\2ab&=2(1)(-2)\\\\2ab&=-4\\end{align*}$$\nAll four terms agree, so $a=1$ and $b=-2$.",
